KG42.register("nodes-5a1c06a734d7a05e3064b455",{"id":"COMP-2bf6582a237ae8bd","code":"CLR033","label":"Local clearing under distribution-line and voltage constraints","definition":"The DSO directly incorporates distribution line and node voltage limits into local market resource allocation, and forms a clearing result that satisfies the corresponding model's network conditions based on the market inputs specified by the source.","layer":"L5","template_code":"JOC","concept_id":"clearing.local","concept_ids":["clearing.local"],"identity_dimensions":{"definition":"The DSO directly incorporates distribution line and node voltage limits into local market resource allocation, and forms a clearing result that satisfies the corresponding model's network conditions based on the market inputs specified by the source.","object":"Local market resource allocation within the distribution network; the product and demand-response objects retain source-specific limitations.","inputs":"Line and voltage operating limits and the corresponding market inputs; 18 retains the given TLMP, and 40 retains demand forecasts, declared costs, and flexibility bounds.","operation":"In the allocation solution, line and voltage feasibility conditions are imposed simultaneously, and the source's product delivery requirements and economic criteria are enforced.","outputs":"Local resource allocation under the model's network conditions; 40 specifically outputs aggregator flexibility instructions.","boundary":"Limited to L5/JOC network-constrained clearing; excludes configurations with only internal energy conservation. The reserve deliverability requirement of 18 and the minimization of the maximum declared cost of 40 are retained separately; price inputs are not unified, and SOCP relaxation exactness or actual fulfillment is not guaranteed."},"exclusions":[["In the general narrative, the corresponding transmission-node TLMP is a given input, and the scope is the low-voltage local market.","Active power, reactive power, reserve, and distributed reserve deliverability are explicitly limited to the Caramanis scheme and are not generalized to all cited literature.","The nodal active-power DLMP is an accompanying price output; on that basis, price formation cannot be incorporated into the shared L5 operation.","No complete objective function or network equations are given, and this is not filled in as the minimax model of 40."],["It is initiated when the network check fails and aggregator bids have been obtained; the object is demand-response flexibility and is not expanded to an arbitrary energy buying or selling role.","Retain min max_i c_i and the implementation problem min z, z≥c_i; the declared cost is Σ_t λ̂_{i,t}[P^D_{i,t}(1−K_{i,t})]^2.","Retain the declared lower and upper bounds K̂_lower≤K≤K̂_upper, the cross-period instructions, and the distinction between declared parameters and true parameters.","The original problem (9) uses constraints (1)–(8); the implementation problem (11) replaces equality (4) with cone inequality (10), and their feasible regions are not equivalent.","P, Q, V, and I are network configuration variables; this is not grounds to determine that this market trades reactive power or reserve products.","K_i^* is the clearing instruction, and the actual implemented quantity may differ from it; the CPM/ECPM formulas reference this output to calculate compensation and do not define a shared allocation operation."]],"members":[{"id":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"DSOs perform local market clearing in the low-voltage grid. Scope: Low-voltage local markets in the common TSO-DSO approach described in the paper. Condition: Given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses. Low-voltage clearing must allow reserves to be delivered by distributed resources while respecting distribution line and voltage limits. Scope: The low-voltage markets proposed by Caramanis et al., clearing real power, reactive power and reserves.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Low-voltage local markets in the common TSO-DSO approach described in the paper. | The low-voltage markets proposed by Caramanis et al., clearing real power, reactive power and reserves.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E002","v141-b-g01-E003"]},"constraint_refs":["18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"75139dbc71609c311251eb9770c6a758341dee18251ca0230b6972546056e166","paper":"18-dlmp_analysis","rule_ref":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001","node_id":"clearing.local","function":"clearing.local","unit_id":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R002::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"Clearing low-voltage local markets determines real-power DLMPs at individual distribution nodes. Scope: The common approach described for [4], [7], and [11]–[14]: real-power pricing at individual distribution nodes in low-voltage local markets. Condition: Given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"The common approach described for [4], [7], and [11]–[14]: real-power pricing at individual distribution nodes in low-voltage local markets.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E003"]},"constraint_refs":["18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"0ff0dba0bb11745bc1fb5fac133fd450c458879a2aac80995ce29cf015d4c707","paper":"18-dlmp_analysis","rule_ref":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R002","node_id":"clearing.local","function":"clearing.local","unit_id":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The DSO performs local flexibility allocation by solving problem (11) given the aggregators’ bids. Scope: Proposed flexibility-market allocation among aggregators within the modeled distribution system. Condition: The network-constraint check fails and aggregators have submitted flexibility bids. The allocation objective is to minimize the highest aggregator flexibility cost while satisfying the distribution system’s operational constraints. Scope: Max-min fair flexibility-allocation optimization within the modeled distribution system.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Max-min fair flexibility-allocation optimization within the modeled distribution system. | Proposed flexibility-market allocation among aggregators within the modeled distribution system.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E19"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C003","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C004","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C005","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C006"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"e03dd623fdbb063aed9525656a6b9287e2943202a2b70919f533e9166984a2dc","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001","node_id":"clearing.local","function":"clearing.local","unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"The optimal local flexibility-allocation instructions feed compensation calculation: other aggregators' declared costs are evaluated at their instructions for the maximum-cost penalty, and the recipient's implemented flexibility is compared with its instruction for the deviation penalty. Measured consumption and proof of payments also enter the calculation. Scope: Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. The DSO designs the aggregator compensation function and chooses its penalization factor beta. Scope: DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. ECPM includes a fixed payment to each aggregator intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff. Scope: Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. ECPM compensation includes reimbursement of the aggregator's actual flexibility cost as a component, alongside its penalty deductions. Scope: Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. The deviation penalty is quadratic in deviation volume and applies in both upward and downward directions. Its per-unit penalization increases with deviation magnitude, strongly penalizing large deviations while keeping small, possibly unintended deviations mild. Scope: Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction. Condition: An aggregator implements flexibility that deviates from the DSO instruction. Deviation penalties must be high enough to deter misreporting while avoiding levels that make aggregators with uncertainty prefer to opt out because of potential negative payoffs and heavy penalties. Scope: ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. | DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. | ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty. | Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. | Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. | Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E18","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-E21"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"e2e1bbf6c09250fdda3484fd4e5edbd04edd283e47fe691fc6021e384f76eaa3","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003","node_id":"clearing.local","function":"clearing.local","unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false}],"paper_ids":["18-dlmp_analysis","40-fair_dso_flexibility"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E003","location":{"location_id":"src-b1966ef5f7-p01-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"ure p , p , he such that the reserves can be delivered by distributed resources uf- while respecting","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A related question is how distribution locational marginal prices should be computed. In this respect, the common approach adopted in [4], [7], and [11]–[14] is the following: (i) locational marginal prices of high-voltage buses (TLMPs) are determined by clearing transmission markets, and (ii) DSOs clear local markets in the low-voltage grid in order to determine distribution locational marginal prices (DLMPs) for real power at the level of individual distribution nodes, given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses.","paper":"18-dlmp_analysis","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"A related question is how distribution locational marginal prices should be computed. In this respect, the common approach adopted in [4], [7], and [11]–[14] is the following: (i) locational marginal prices of high-voltage buses (TLMPs) are determined by clearing transmission markets, and (ii) DSOs clear local markets in the low-voltage grid in order to determine distribution locational marginal prices (DLMPs) for real power at the level of individual distribution nodes, given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E002","location":{"location_id":"src-b1966ef5f7-p01-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"aspect of electricity market design. The relevance of dis- tributed resources is expected to increas","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Caramanis et al. [4] propose (i) a transmission system market that trades real power and reserves, and (ii) low-voltage markets that clear real power, reactive power and reserves, such that the reserves can be delivered by distributed resources while respecting distribution line limits and voltage limits.","paper":"18-dlmp_analysis","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"Caramanis et al. [4] propose (i) a transmission system market that trades real power and reserves, and (ii) low-voltage markets that clear real power, reactive power and reserves, such that the reserves can be delivered by distributed resources while respecting distribution line limits and voltage limits."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E003","location":{"location_id":"src-b1966ef5f7-p01-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"ure p , p , he such that the reserves can be delivered by distributed resources uf- while respecting","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A related question is how distribution locational marginal prices should be computed. In this respect, the common approach adopted in [4], [7], and [11]–[14] is the following: (i) locational marginal prices of high-voltage buses (TLMPs) are determined by clearing transmission markets, and (ii) DSOs clear local markets in the low-voltage grid in order to determine distribution locational marginal prices (DLMPs) for real power at the level of individual distribution nodes, given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses.","paper":"18-dlmp_analysis","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"A related question is how distribution locational marginal prices should be computed. In this respect, the common approach adopted in [4], [7], and [11]–[14] is the following: (i) locational marginal prices of high-voltage buses (TLMPs) are determined by clearing transmission markets, and (ii) DSOs clear local markets in the low-voltage grid in order to determine distribution locational marginal prices (DLMPs) for real power at the level of individual distribution nodes, given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"2252 PDi,t(1 −Ki,t)), which comes at a cost deﬁned as 2 ci(Ki, Li) = λi,t PDi,t 1 −Ki,t ∀i ∈ b. (8) ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective of the DSO is to satisfy the operational constraints of the distribution system, while minimizing the highest flexibility cost incurred among aggregators by deciding the optimal flexibility activation. In other words, the DSO pursues a fair allocation of flexibility costs among aggregators so that all aggregators are motivated to contribute, while no particular aggregator has to be compromised to unilaterally facilitate the DSO in achieving its objectives.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"The objective of the DSO is to satisfy the operational constraints of the distribution system, while minimizing the highest flexibility cost incurred among aggregators by deciding the optimal flexibility activation. In other words, the DSO pursues a fair allocation of flexibility costs among aggregators so that all aggregators are motivated to contribute, while no particular aggregator has to be compromised to unilaterally facilitate the DSO in achieving its objectives."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p02-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. INTRODUCTION AND RELATED WORK","anchor_text":"2250 locally by aggregators in order to keep the network within [1 safe operational limits. me In th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this article we consider a distribution system and a set of aggregators, where each aggregator represents a set of loads along with a set of flexibility assets. The second-order cone programming relaxation based on the branch flow model as in [4], [5] has been used to represent the operation of the distribution system. Given the day-ahead program, the DSO calls on the aggregators’ flexibility in order to satisfy the physical network’s constraints. An aggregator provides flexibility by modifying its demand profile with respect to its day-ahead market program.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"In this article we consider a distribution system and a set of aggregators, where each aggregator represents a set of loads along with a set of flexibility assets. The second-order cone programming relaxation based on the branch flow model as in [4], [5] has been used to represent the operation of the distribution system. Given the day-ahead program, the DSO calls on the aggregators’ flexibility in order to satisfy the physical network’s constraints. An aggregator provides flexibility by modifying its demand profile with respect to its day-ahead market program."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"pursues a fair allocation of ﬂexibility costs among aggre gators so that all aggregators are motivat","equation_id":"(10)-(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"By introducing the slack variable \\(z\\), and replacing (4) with \\(V^{sqr}_{j,t}I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\ge P_{ij,t}^2+Q_{ij,t}^2\\quad \\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (10)\\) problem (9) can be recast as second order cone programming problem that is represented by \\(\\min_{z,K_i,V^{sqr}_{i,t},I^{sqr}_{ij,t},P_{ij,t},Q_{ij,t},P^S_{0,t},Q^S_{0,t}}\\{z\\}\\) s.t. \\(z\\ge c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\ \\forall i\\in\\Omega_b\\); \\((1)\\text{–}(3), (5)\\text{–}(8),\\ \\text{and}\\ (10)\\quad (11)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"By introducing the slack variable \\(z\\), and replacing (4) with \\(V^{sqr}_{j,t}I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\ge P_{ij,t}^2+Q_{ij,t}^2\\quad \\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (10)\\) problem (9) can be recast as second order cone programming problem that is represented by \\(\\min_{z,K_i,V^{sqr}_{i,t},I^{sqr}_{ij,t},P_{ij,t},Q_{ij,t},P^S_{0,t},Q^S_{0,t}}\\{z\\}\\) s.t. \\(z\\ge c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\ \\forall i\\in\\Omega_b\\); \\((1)\\text{–}(3), (5)\\text{–}(8),\\ \\text{and}\\ (10)\\quad (11)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\{\\hat\\lambda_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\}\\) denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\(\\mathbf L_i\\) which may or may not be equal to the actual \\(\\mathbf L_i\\). The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"Let \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\{\\hat\\lambda_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\}\\) denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\(\\mathbf L_i\\) which may or may not be equal to the actual \\(\\mathbf L_i\\). The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"2252 PDi,t(1 −Ki,t)), which comes at a cost deﬁned as 2 ci(Ki, Li) = λi,t PDi,t 1 −Ki,t ∀i ∈ b. (8) ","equation_id":"(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"This is modelled by causing a deviation in the prosumers’ active power demand (from \\(P^D_{i,t}\\) to \\(P^D_{i,t}(1-K_{i,t})\\)), which comes at a cost defined as \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)=\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\lambda_{i,t}\\left(P^D_{i,t}(1-K_{i,t})\\right)^2\\quad \\forall i\\in\\Omega_b.\\) (8)","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"This is modelled by causing a deviation in the prosumers’ active power demand (from \\(P^D_{i,t}\\) to \\(P^D_{i,t}(1-K_{i,t})\\)), which comes at a cost defined as \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)=\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\lambda_{i,t}\\left(P^D_{i,t}(1-K_{i,t})\\right)^2\\quad \\forall i\\in\\Omega_b.\\) (8)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the design of the reward function \\(p_i\\) we consider the family of mechanisms referred to as compensation and penalty mechanisms (CPM), as in [36, Sec. 10.6.1]. In our context, the aggregators’ instructions \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\), are calculated by solving problem (11), but using the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as the input that defines the functions \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\). After the observation of the outcome, the aggregator’s reward is calculated by \\(p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}\\quad (13)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"For the design of the reward function \\(p_i\\) we consider the family of mechanisms referred to as compensation and penalty mechanisms (CPM), as in [36, Sec. 10.6.1]. In our context, the aggregators’ instructions \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\), are calculated by solving problem (11), but using the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as the input that defines the functions \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\). After the observation of the outcome, the aggregator’s reward is calculated by \\(p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}\\quad (13)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E30","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL","anchor_text":"V2 ≤Vsqri,t ≤V2 ∀i ∈ b, t ∈ T (5) b 0 ≤Isqrij,t ≤I2ij ∀ij ∈ l, t ∈ T (6) der An aggregator’s ﬂexibil","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\mathbf K_i=\\{K_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}\\) denote \\(i\\)’s profile of flexibility activation. Accordingly, tuple \\(\\mathbf L_i=\\{\\lambda_{i,t},\\overline K_{i,t},\\underline K_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}\\) denotes the aggregator’s local parameters, where \\(\\lambda_{i,t}\\) is a cost parameter for flexibility activation. It is assumed that the aggregator draws its flexibility by calling on demand-response resources from the prosumers in its portfolio at a cost defined by function \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"Let \\(\\mathbf K_i=\\{K_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}\\) denote \\(i\\)’s profile of flexibility activation. Accordingly, tuple \\(\\mathbf L_i=\\{\\lambda_{i,t},\\overline K_{i,t},\\underline K_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}\\) denotes the aggregator’s local parameters, where \\(\\lambda_{i,t}\\) is a cost parameter for flexibility activation. It is assumed that the aggregator draws its flexibility by calling on demand-response resources from the prosumers in its portfolio at a cost defined by function \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"pursues a fair allocation of ﬂexibility costs among aggre gators so that all aggregators are motivat","equation_id":"(9)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\min_{K_i,V^{sqr}_{i,t},I^{sqr}_{ij,t},P_{ij,t},Q_{ij,t},P^S_{0,t},Q^S_{0,t}}\\ \\max_{i\\in\\Omega_b}\\{c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\} \\quad \\text{s.t.}\\ (1)\\text{–}(8) \\quad (9)","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"\\min_{K_i,V^{sqr}_{i,t},I^{sqr}_{ij,t},P_{ij,t},Q_{ij,t},P^S_{0,t},Q^S_{0,t}}\\ \\max_{i\\in\\Omega_b}\\{c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\} \\quad \\text{s.t.}\\ (1)\\text{–}(8) \\quad (9)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E29","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL","anchor_text":"presented. Since current and voltage magnitudes appear naturally as hat squared, non-negative, conti","equation_id":"(3)-(7)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Since current and voltage magnitudes appear naturally as squared, non-negative, continuous variables in the steady-state operation of AC electrical distribution systems, it is convenient to perform the change in variables \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\equiv V_{i,t}^2\\) and \\(I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\equiv I_{ij,t}^2\\) as in [35]. The voltage magnitude drop between nodes \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) is represented by \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}-2(R_{ij}P_{ij,t}+X_{ij}Q_{ij,t})-(R_{ij}^2+X_{ij}^2)I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=V^{sqr}_{j,t},\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (3)\\), while current magnitudes are calculated using \\(V^{sqr}_{j,t}I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=P_{ij,t}^2+Q_{ij,t}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (4)\\). Upper and lower bounds on nodal voltage magnitudes and current magnitudes are enforced by \\(\\underline V^2\\le V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\le\\overline V^2,\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (5)\\) and \\(0\\le I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\le\\overline I_{ij}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (6)\\). An aggregator’s flexibility is bounded as in \\(\\underline K_{i,t}\\le K_{i,t}\\le\\overline K_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (7)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"Since current and voltage magnitudes appear naturally as squared, non-negative, continuous variables in the steady-state operation of AC electrical distribution systems, it is convenient to perform the change in variables \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\equiv V_{i,t}^2\\) and \\(I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\equiv I_{ij,t}^2\\) as in [35]. The voltage magnitude drop between nodes \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) is represented by \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}-2(R_{ij}P_{ij,t}+X_{ij}Q_{ij,t})-(R_{ij}^2+X_{ij}^2)I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=V^{sqr}_{j,t},\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (3)\\), while current magnitudes are calculated using \\(V^{sqr}_{j,t}I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=P_{ij,t}^2+Q_{ij,t}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (4)\\). Upper and lower bounds on nodal voltage magnitudes and current magnitudes are enforced by \\(\\underline V^2\\le V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\le\\overline V^2,\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (5)\\) and \\(0\\le I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\le\\overline I_{ij}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (6)\\). An aggregator’s flexibility is bounded as in \\(\\underline K_{i,t}\\le K_{i,t}\\le\\overline K_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (7)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\{\\hat\\lambda_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\}\\) denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\(\\mathbf L_i\\) which may or may not be equal to the actual \\(\\mathbf L_i\\). The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Let \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\{\\hat\\lambda_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\}\\) denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\(\\mathbf L_i\\) which may or may not be equal to the actual \\(\\mathbf L_i\\). The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, in practice, aggregators are not hard-constrained to follow the instruction. In fact, the aggregator can instead opt for a profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne\\mathbf K_i^*\\). In this case, the original CPM mechanism is not enough to guarantee incentive compatibility. We propose a novel payment rule, called enhanced-CPM (ECPM), modifying the CPM rule as stated by \\(p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2\\quad (14)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"However, in practice, aggregators are not hard-constrained to follow the instruction. In fact, the aggregator can instead opt for a profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne\\mathbf K_i^*\\). In this case, the original CPM mechanism is not enough to guarantee incentive compatibility. We propose a novel payment rule, called enhanced-CPM (ECPM), modifying the CPM rule as stated by \\(p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2\\quad (14)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Once the instruction is communicated to each aggregator for the horizon \\(\\Omega_T\\), real-time operation begins. The aggregator implements a flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), where the tilde denotes the fact that this is the actual final flexibility factor implemented by the aggregator, as measured by the DSO’s smart meters. In real time operation, two things may go differently than scheduled: • The aggregator might not follow the instruction, and implement a different final profile, i.e., \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne \\mathbf K_i^*\\). • The actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\) that the aggregator pays to its customers for the implementation of the flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), may not be equal to the cost calculated by the DSO, \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\), e.g., aggregators providing false declarations of flexibility costs. Note that both cases result in inefficiency, since even after assuming that the aggregator perfectly follows the instruction, the costs can still differ, i.e., \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\mathbf L_i)\\ne c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Once the instruction is communicated to each aggregator for the horizon \\(\\Omega_T\\), real-time operation begins. The aggregator implements a flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), where the tilde denotes the fact that this is the actual final flexibility factor implemented by the aggregator, as measured by the DSO’s smart meters. In real time operation, two things may go differently than scheduled: • The aggregator might not follow the instruction, and implement a different final profile, i.e., \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne \\mathbf K_i^*\\). • The actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\) that the aggregator pays to its customers for the implementation of the flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), may not be equal to the cost calculated by the DSO, \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\), e.g., aggregators providing false declarations of flexibility costs. Note that both cases result in inefficiency, since even after assuming that the aggregator perfectly follows the instruction, the costs can still differ, i.e., \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\mathbf L_i)\\ne c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}},\\hat{\\mathbf{L}})=R_i+\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\mathbb{T}}}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde{K}_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2\\qquad (14)$$","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"$$p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}},\\hat{\\mathbf{L}})=R_i+\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\mathbb{T}}}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde{K}_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2\\qquad (14)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term, R_i, is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\gamma, that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild. The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The first term, R_i, is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\gamma, that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild. The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the design of the reward function \\(p_i\\) we consider the family of mechanisms referred to as compensation and penalty mechanisms (CPM), as in [36, Sec. 10.6.1]. In our context, the aggregators’ instructions \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\), are calculated by solving problem (11), but using the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as the input that defines the functions \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\). After the observation of the outcome, the aggregator’s reward is calculated by \\(p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}\\quad (13)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"For the design of the reward function \\(p_i\\) we consider the family of mechanisms referred to as compensation and penalty mechanisms (CPM), as in [36, Sec. 10.6.1]. In our context, the aggregators’ instructions \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\), are calculated by solving problem (11), but using the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as the input that defines the functions \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\). After the observation of the outcome, the aggregator’s reward is calculated by \\(p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}\\quad (13)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}\\qquad (13)$$","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"$$p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}\\qquad (13)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j and instruction factors \\mathbf{K}_j^* of the rest of the aggregators, j\\neq i, but over the observed cost for i. Parameter \\beta is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j and instruction factors \\mathbf{K}_j^* of the rest of the aggregators, j\\neq i, but over the observed cost for i. Parameter \\beta is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p09-c1r4__v141-a-M01-validation","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"APPENDIX A","anchor_text":"APPENDIX A PROOF OF LEMMA 1 With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff πi from (12), considerin","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff \\pi_i from (12), considering K_i = K_i^* is given by \\pi_i = -\\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b,j\\neq i}\\left\\{c_j(K_j^*,\\hat L_j)\\right\\}\\right\\} \\tag{18}","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff \\pi_i from (12), considering K_i = K_i^* is given by \\pi_i = -\\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b,j\\neq i}\\left\\{c_j(K_j^*,\\hat L_j)\\right\\}\\right\\} \\tag{18}"}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["minimax_cost_flexibility_allocation","network_constrained_local_clearing"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L5-885c189932","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L5","ontological_kind":"network_constrained_local_clearing"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"The DSO directly incorporates distribution line and node voltage limits into local market resource allocation, and forms a clearing result that satisfies the corresponding model's network conditions based on the market inputs specified by the source.","object":"Local market resource allocation within the distribution network; the product and demand-response objects retain source-specific limitations.","inputs":"Line and voltage operating limits and the corresponding market inputs; 18 retains the given TLMP, and 40 retains demand forecasts, declared costs, and flexibility bounds.","operation":"In the allocation solution, line and voltage feasibility conditions are imposed simultaneously, and the source's product delivery requirements and economic criteria are enforced.","outputs":"Local resource allocation under the model's network conditions; 40 specifically outputs aggregator flexibility instructions.","boundary":"Limited to L5/JOC network-constrained clearing; excludes configurations with only internal energy conservation. The reserve deliverability requirement of 18 and the minimization of the maximum declared cost of 40 are retained separately; price inputs are not unified, and SOCP relaxation exactness or actual fulfillment is not guaranteed."},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","paper_id":"18-dlmp_analysis","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"DSOs perform local market clearing in the low-voltage grid. Scope: Low-voltage local markets in the common TSO-DSO approach described in the paper. Condition: Given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses. Low-voltage clearing must allow reserves to be delivered by distributed resources while respecting distribution line and voltage limits. Scope: The low-voltage markets proposed by Caramanis et al., clearing real power, reactive power and reserves.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Low-voltage local markets in the common TSO-DSO approach described in the paper. | The low-voltage markets proposed by Caramanis et al., clearing real power, reactive power and reserves.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E002","v141-b-g01-E003"]},"constraint_refs":["18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"75139dbc71609c311251eb9770c6a758341dee18251ca0230b6972546056e166"},{"id":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R002::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"Clearing low-voltage local markets determines real-power DLMPs at individual distribution nodes. Scope: The common approach described for [4], [7], and [11]–[14]: real-power pricing at individual distribution nodes in low-voltage local markets. Condition: Given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"The common approach described for [4], [7], and [11]–[14]: real-power pricing at individual distribution nodes in low-voltage local markets.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E003"]},"constraint_refs":["18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"0ff0dba0bb11745bc1fb5fac133fd450c458879a2aac80995ce29cf015d4c707"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"DSOs perform local market clearing in the low-voltage grid. Scope: Low-voltage local markets in the common TSO-DSO approach described in the paper. Condition: Given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E003"],"ref":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E003","location":{"location_id":"src-b1966ef5f7-p01-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"ure p , p , he such that the reserves can be delivered by distributed resources uf- while respecting","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A related question is how distribution locational marginal prices should be computed. In this respect, the common approach adopted in [4], [7], and [11]–[14] is the following: (i) locational marginal prices of high-voltage buses (TLMPs) are determined by clearing transmission markets, and (ii) DSOs clear local markets in the low-voltage grid in order to determine distribution locational marginal prices (DLMPs) for real power at the level of individual distribution nodes, given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Low-voltage clearing must allow reserves to be delivered by distributed resources while respecting distribution line and voltage limits. Scope: The low-voltage markets proposed by Caramanis et al., clearing real power, reactive power and reserves.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E002"],"ref":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E002","location":{"location_id":"src-b1966ef5f7-p01-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"aspect of electricity market design. The relevance of dis- tributed resources is expected to increas","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Caramanis et al. [4] propose (i) a transmission system market that trades real power and reserves, and (ii) low-voltage markets that clear real power, reactive power and reserves, such that the reserves can be delivered by distributed resources while respecting distribution line limits and voltage limits."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Clearing low-voltage local markets determines real-power DLMPs at individual distribution nodes. Scope: The common approach described for [4], [7], and [11]–[14]: real-power pricing at individual distribution nodes in low-voltage local markets. Condition: Given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E003"],"ref":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E003","location":{"location_id":"src-b1966ef5f7-p01-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"ure p , p , he such that the reserves can be delivered by distributed resources uf- while respecting","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A related question is how distribution locational marginal prices should be computed. In this respect, the common approach adopted in [4], [7], and [11]–[14] is the following: (i) locational marginal prices of high-voltage buses (TLMPs) are determined by clearing transmission markets, and (ii) DSOs clear local markets in the low-voltage grid in order to determine distribution locational marginal prices (DLMPs) for real power at the level of individual distribution nodes, given the TLMP of the corresponding transmission buses."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","paper_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The DSO performs local flexibility allocation by solving problem (11) given the aggregators’ bids. Scope: Proposed flexibility-market allocation among aggregators within the modeled distribution system. Condition: The network-constraint check fails and aggregators have submitted flexibility bids. The allocation objective is to minimize the highest aggregator flexibility cost while satisfying the distribution system’s operational constraints. Scope: Max-min fair flexibility-allocation optimization within the modeled distribution system.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Max-min fair flexibility-allocation optimization within the modeled distribution system. | Proposed flexibility-market allocation among aggregators within the modeled distribution system.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E19"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C003","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C004","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C005","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C006"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"e03dd623fdbb063aed9525656a6b9287e2943202a2b70919f533e9166984a2dc"},{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"The optimal local flexibility-allocation instructions feed compensation calculation: other aggregators' declared costs are evaluated at their instructions for the maximum-cost penalty, and the recipient's implemented flexibility is compared with its instruction for the deviation penalty. Measured consumption and proof of payments also enter the calculation. Scope: Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. The DSO designs the aggregator compensation function and chooses its penalization factor beta. Scope: DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. ECPM includes a fixed payment to each aggregator intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff. Scope: Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. ECPM compensation includes reimbursement of the aggregator's actual flexibility cost as a component, alongside its penalty deductions. Scope: Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. The deviation penalty is quadratic in deviation volume and applies in both upward and downward directions. Its per-unit penalization increases with deviation magnitude, strongly penalizing large deviations while keeping small, possibly unintended deviations mild. Scope: Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction. Condition: An aggregator implements flexibility that deviates from the DSO instruction. Deviation penalties must be high enough to deter misreporting while avoiding levels that make aggregators with uncertainty prefer to opt out because of potential negative payoffs and heavy penalties. Scope: ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. | DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. | ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty. | Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. | Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. | Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E18","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-E21"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"e2e1bbf6c09250fdda3484fd4e5edbd04edd283e47fe691fc6021e384f76eaa3"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The DSO performs local flexibility allocation by solving problem (11) given the aggregators’ bids. Scope: Proposed flexibility-market allocation among aggregators within the modeled distribution system. Condition: The network-constraint check fails and aggregators have submitted flexibility bids.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E19"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"2252 PDi,t(1 −Ki,t)), which comes at a cost deﬁned as 2 ci(Ki, Li) = λi,t PDi,t 1 −Ki,t ∀i ∈ b. (8) ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective of the DSO is to satisfy the operational constraints of the distribution system, while minimizing the highest flexibility cost incurred among aggregators by deciding the optimal flexibility activation. In other words, the DSO pursues a fair allocation of flexibility costs among aggregators so that all aggregators are motivated to contribute, while no particular aggregator has to be compromised to unilaterally facilitate the DSO in achieving its objectives."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The allocation objective is to minimize the highest aggregator flexibility cost while satisfying the distribution system’s operational constraints. Scope: Max-min fair flexibility-allocation optimization within the modeled distribution system.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"2252 PDi,t(1 −Ki,t)), which comes at a cost deﬁned as 2 ci(Ki, Li) = λi,t PDi,t 1 −Ki,t ∀i ∈ b. (8) ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective of the DSO is to satisfy the operational constraints of the distribution system, while minimizing the highest flexibility cost incurred among aggregators by deciding the optimal flexibility activation. In other words, the DSO pursues a fair allocation of flexibility costs among aggregators so that all aggregators are motivated to contribute, while no particular aggregator has to be compromised to unilaterally facilitate the DSO in achieving its objectives."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Given the aggregators' declared bid tuples, the DSO's clearing instruction is the solution of the SOCP slack-variable max-min allocation problem (11): minimize z subject to z being at least each aggregator's declared-cost flexibility cost and to the network and flexibility constraints (1)-(3), (5)-(8), and (10), with (7) evaluated at the declared flexibility bounds.","expression":"\\min_{z,\\mathbf K_i,V^{sqr}_{i,t},I^{sqr}_{ij,t},P_{ij,t},Q_{ij,t},P^S_{0,t},Q^S_{0,t}}\\{z\\}\\quad\\text{s.t. } z\\ge c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\ \\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,\\quad (1)-(3),(5)-(8),\\text{ and }(10)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R001-X001","v141-c-R001-X002","v141-c-R001-X003","v141-c-R001-X004","v141-c-R001-X005","v141-c-R001-X006","v141-c-R001-X007","v141-c-R001-X008"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R001-T001","v141-c-R001-T002","v141-c-R001-T003","v141-c-R001-T004","v141-c-R001-T005","v141-c-R001-T006","v141-c-R001-T007","v141-c-R001-T008","v141-c-R001-T009","v141-c-R001-T010"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E19"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p02-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. INTRODUCTION AND RELATED WORK","anchor_text":"2250 locally by aggregators in order to keep the network within [1 safe operational limits. me In th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this article we consider a distribution system and a set of aggregators, where each aggregator represents a set of loads along with a set of flexibility assets. The second-order cone programming relaxation based on the branch flow model as in [4], [5] has been used to represent the operation of the distribution system. Given the day-ahead program, the DSO calls on the aggregators’ flexibility in order to satisfy the physical network’s constraints. An aggregator provides flexibility by modifying its demand profile with respect to its day-ahead market program."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"pursues a fair allocation of ﬂexibility costs among aggre gators so that all aggregators are motivat","equation_id":"(10)-(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"By introducing the slack variable \\(z\\), and replacing (4) with \\(V^{sqr}_{j,t}I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\ge P_{ij,t}^2+Q_{ij,t}^2\\quad \\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (10)\\) problem (9) can be recast as second order cone programming problem that is represented by \\(\\min_{z,K_i,V^{sqr}_{i,t},I^{sqr}_{ij,t},P_{ij,t},Q_{ij,t},P^S_{0,t},Q^S_{0,t}}\\{z\\}\\) s.t. \\(z\\ge c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\ \\forall i\\in\\Omega_b\\); \\((1)\\text{–}(3), (5)\\text{–}(8),\\ \\text{and}\\ (10)\\quad (11)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\{\\hat\\lambda_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\}\\) denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\(\\mathbf L_i\\) which may or may not be equal to the actual \\(\\mathbf L_i\\). The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The declared-cost flexibility cost function used by the DSO in the clearing problem is the horizon sum of the declared cost parameter times the squared demand reduction implied by the flexibility factor.","expression":"c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)=\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\hat{\\lambda}_{i,t}\\left(P^D_{i,t}(1-K_{i,t})\\right)^2\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R001-X001"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R001-T001","v141-c-R001-T004"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g01-E20","v141-b-g01-E30"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"2252 PDi,t(1 −Ki,t)), which comes at a cost deﬁned as 2 ci(Ki, Li) = λi,t PDi,t 1 −Ki,t ∀i ∈ b. (8) ","equation_id":"(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"This is modelled by causing a deviation in the prosumers’ active power demand (from \\(P^D_{i,t}\\) to \\(P^D_{i,t}(1-K_{i,t})\\)), which comes at a cost defined as \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)=\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\lambda_{i,t}\\left(P^D_{i,t}(1-K_{i,t})\\right)^2\\quad \\forall i\\in\\Omega_b.\\) (8)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the design of the reward function \\(p_i\\) we consider the family of mechanisms referred to as compensation and penalty mechanisms (CPM), as in [36, Sec. 10.6.1]. In our context, the aggregators’ instructions \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\), are calculated by solving problem (11), but using the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as the input that defines the functions \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\). After the observation of the outcome, the aggregator’s reward is calculated by \\(p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}\\quad (13)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E30","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL","anchor_text":"V2 ≤Vsqri,t ≤V2 ∀i ∈ b, t ∈ T (5) b 0 ≤Isqrij,t ≤I2ij ∀ij ∈ l, t ∈ T (6) der An aggregator’s ﬂexibil","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\mathbf K_i=\\{K_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}\\) denote \\(i\\)’s profile of flexibility activation. Accordingly, tuple \\(\\mathbf L_i=\\{\\lambda_{i,t},\\overline K_{i,t},\\underline K_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}\\) denotes the aggregator’s local parameters, where \\(\\lambda_{i,t}\\) is a cost parameter for flexibility activation. It is assumed that the aggregator draws its flexibility by calling on demand-response resources from the prosumers in its portfolio at a cost defined by function \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Given the aggregators' declared bid tuples, the DSO's allocation objective is the max-min fair objective: minimize the highest declared-cost flexibility cost among aggregators, subject to the operational constraints (1)-(8).","expression":"\\min_{\\mathbf K_i,V^{sqr}_{i,t},I^{sqr}_{ij,t},P_{ij,t},Q_{ij,t},P^S_{0,t},Q^S_{0,t}}\\ \\max_{i\\in\\Omega_b}\\{c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\} \\quad\\text{s.t. }(1)-(8)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R001-X001","v141-c-R001-X003","v141-c-R001-X004","v141-c-R001-X005","v141-c-R001-X006","v141-c-R001-X007","v141-c-R001-X008"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R001-T001","v141-c-R001-T002","v141-c-R001-T003","v141-c-R001-T004","v141-c-R001-T005","v141-c-R001-T006","v141-c-R001-T007","v141-c-R001-T008","v141-c-R001-T009","v141-c-R001-T010"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E11","v141-b-g01-E19"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"2252 PDi,t(1 −Ki,t)), which comes at a cost deﬁned as 2 ci(Ki, Li) = λi,t PDi,t 1 −Ki,t ∀i ∈ b. (8) ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective of the DSO is to satisfy the operational constraints of the distribution system, while minimizing the highest flexibility cost incurred among aggregators by deciding the optimal flexibility activation. In other words, the DSO pursues a fair allocation of flexibility costs among aggregators so that all aggregators are motivated to contribute, while no particular aggregator has to be compromised to unilaterally facilitate the DSO in achieving its objectives."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"pursues a fair allocation of ﬂexibility costs among aggre gators so that all aggregators are motivat","equation_id":"(9)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\min_{K_i,V^{sqr}_{i,t},I^{sqr}_{ij,t},P_{ij,t},Q_{ij,t},P^S_{0,t},Q^S_{0,t}}\\ \\max_{i\\in\\Omega_b}\\{c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\} \\quad \\text{s.t.}\\ (1)\\text{–}(8) \\quad (9)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C006","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"When problem (11) is solved from the aggregators' declarations, the flexibility factor is bounded by the declared minimum and maximum flexibility factors.","expression":"\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\le K_{i,t}\\le\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t}\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R001-X001"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R001-T002","v141-c-R001-T003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\{\\hat\\lambda_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\}\\) denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\(\\mathbf L_i\\) which may or may not be equal to the actual \\(\\mathbf L_i\\). The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E29","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL","anchor_text":"presented. Since current and voltage magnitudes appear naturally as hat squared, non-negative, conti","equation_id":"(3)-(7)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Since current and voltage magnitudes appear naturally as squared, non-negative, continuous variables in the steady-state operation of AC electrical distribution systems, it is convenient to perform the change in variables \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\equiv V_{i,t}^2\\) and \\(I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\equiv I_{ij,t}^2\\) as in [35]. The voltage magnitude drop between nodes \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) is represented by \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}-2(R_{ij}P_{ij,t}+X_{ij}Q_{ij,t})-(R_{ij}^2+X_{ij}^2)I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=V^{sqr}_{j,t},\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (3)\\), while current magnitudes are calculated using \\(V^{sqr}_{j,t}I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=P_{ij,t}^2+Q_{ij,t}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (4)\\). Upper and lower bounds on nodal voltage magnitudes and current magnitudes are enforced by \\(\\underline V^2\\le V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\le\\overline V^2,\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (5)\\) and \\(0\\le I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\le\\overline I_{ij}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (6)\\). An aggregator’s flexibility is bounded as in \\(\\underline K_{i,t}\\le K_{i,t}\\le\\overline K_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (7)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E30","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL","anchor_text":"V2 ≤Vsqri,t ≤V2 ∀i ∈ b, t ∈ T (5) b 0 ≤Isqrij,t ≤I2ij ∀ij ∈ l, t ∈ T (6) der An aggregator’s ﬂexibil","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\mathbf K_i=\\{K_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}\\) denote \\(i\\)’s profile of flexibility activation. Accordingly, tuple \\(\\mathbf L_i=\\{\\lambda_{i,t},\\overline K_{i,t},\\underline K_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}\\) denotes the aggregator’s local parameters, where \\(\\lambda_{i,t}\\) is a cost parameter for flexibility activation. It is assumed that the aggregator draws its flexibility by calling on demand-response resources from the prosumers in its portfolio at a cost defined by function \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The optimal local flexibility-allocation instructions feed compensation calculation: other aggregators' declared costs are evaluated at their instructions for the maximum-cost penalty, and the recipient's implemented flexibility is compared with its instruction for the deviation penalty. Measured consumption and proof of payments also enter the calculation. Scope: Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\{\\hat\\lambda_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\}\\) denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\(\\mathbf L_i\\) which may or may not be equal to the actual \\(\\mathbf L_i\\). The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"ECPM payment rule (14): the payment to aggregator i equals the fixed payment R_i plus the observed actual cost, minus beta times the maximum of the observed actual cost and the worst declared cost among other aggregators evaluated at their instructions, minus a quadratic deviation penalty summed over the horizon.","expression":"p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L}) = R_i + \\tilde c_i - \\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\right\\} - \\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["var_beta","var_gamma","var_R_i","var_tildeK_i"],"parameter_refs":["par_K_i_star","par_tilde_c_i","par_hatL_j","par_c_j_eval","par_Omega_b","par_Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E17"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, in practice, aggregators are not hard-constrained to follow the instruction. In fact, the aggregator can instead opt for a profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne\\mathbf K_i^*\\). In this case, the original CPM mechanism is not enough to guarantee incentive compatibility. We propose a novel payment rule, called enhanced-CPM (ECPM), modifying the CPM rule as stated by \\(p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2\\quad (14)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Once the instruction is communicated to each aggregator for the horizon \\(\\Omega_T\\), real-time operation begins. The aggregator implements a flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), where the tilde denotes the fact that this is the actual final flexibility factor implemented by the aggregator, as measured by the DSO’s smart meters. In real time operation, two things may go differently than scheduled: • The aggregator might not follow the instruction, and implement a different final profile, i.e., \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne \\mathbf K_i^*\\). • The actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\) that the aggregator pays to its customers for the implementation of the flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), may not be equal to the cost calculated by the DSO, \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\), e.g., aggregators providing false declarations of flexibility costs. Note that both cases result in inefficiency, since even after assuming that the aggregator perfectly follows the instruction, the costs can still differ, i.e., \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\mathbf L_i)\\ne c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}},\\hat{\\mathbf{L}})=R_i+\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\mathbb{T}}}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde{K}_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2\\qquad (14)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term, R_i, is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\gamma, that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild. The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C008","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"Inherited CPM payment rule (13): the CPM reward equals observed actual cost minus beta times the maximum of the observed actual cost and the maximum declared cost of other aggregators evaluated at their instructions.","expression":"p_i^{CPM} = \\tilde c_i - \\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\right\\}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["var_beta"],"parameter_refs":["par_tilde_c_i","par_K_i_star","par_hatL_j","par_c_j_eval","par_Omega_b"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E20","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g02-E11","v141-b-g02-E12","v141-b-g03-E07"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the design of the reward function \\(p_i\\) we consider the family of mechanisms referred to as compensation and penalty mechanisms (CPM), as in [36, Sec. 10.6.1]. In our context, the aggregators’ instructions \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\), are calculated by solving problem (11), but using the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as the input that defines the functions \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\). After the observation of the outcome, the aggregator’s reward is calculated by \\(p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}\\quad (13)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}\\qquad (13)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j and instruction factors \\mathbf{K}_j^* of the rest of the aggregators, j\\neq i, but over the observed cost for i. Parameter \\beta is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p09-c1r4__v141-a-M01-validation","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"APPENDIX A","anchor_text":"APPENDIX A PROOF OF LEMMA 1 With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff πi from (12), considerin","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff \\pi_i from (12), considering K_i = K_i^* is given by \\pi_i = -\\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b,j\\neq i}\\left\\{c_j(K_j^*,\\hat L_j)\\right\\}\\right\\} \\tag{18}"}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R001-X001","symbol":"\\mathbf K_i=\\{K_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}","meaning":"Profile of flexibility activation/factor for aggregator i chosen by the DSO in clearing; K_{i,t} modifies active power demand P^D_{i,t}.","kind":"operational","domain":"Each K_{i,t} is bounded by the flexibility limits (7): \\underline K_{i,t}\\le K_{i,t}\\le\\overline K_{i,t}.","unit":null,"indices":["i\\in\\Omega_b","t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g01-E33","v141-b-g01-E15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::x::v141-c-R001-X001"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R001-X002","symbol":"z","meaning":"Slack variable introduced to recast the max-min objective; z is minimized subject to z being at least each aggregator's flexibility cost.","kind":"operational","domain":"Auxiliary scalar in (11), constrained by z\\ge c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i) for all i\\in\\Omega_b.","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g01-E04"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::x::v141-c-R001-X002"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R001-X003","symbol":"V^{sqr}_{i,t}","meaning":"Squared voltage magnitude at node i, period t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Non-negative continuous variable satisfying (5): \\underline V^2\\le V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\le\\overline V^2.","unit":"kV^2","indices":["i\\in\\Omega_b","t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E33"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::x::v141-c-R001-X003"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R001-X004","symbol":"I^{sqr}_{ij,t}","meaning":"Squared current magnitude on line ij, period t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Non-negative continuous variable satisfying (6) and the SOC constraint (10): 0\\le I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\le\\overline I_{ij}^2 and V^{sqr}_{j,t}I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\ge P_{ij,t}^2+Q_{ij,t}^2.","unit":"A^2","indices":["ij\\in\\Omega_l","t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g01-E33"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::x::v141-c-R001-X004"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R001-X005","symbol":"P_{ij,t}","meaning":"Active power flow on line ij, period t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Line-flow variable in the clearing program; no explicit sign bound is stated in the quoted equations.","unit":"kW","indices":["ij\\in\\Omega_l","t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E33"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::x::v141-c-R001-X005"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R001-X006","symbol":"Q_{ij,t}","meaning":"Reactive power flow on line ij, period t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Line-flow variable in the clearing program; no explicit sign bound is stated in the quoted equations.","unit":"kvar","indices":["ij\\in\\Omega_l","t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E33"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::x::v141-c-R001-X006"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R001-X007","symbol":"P^S_{0,t}","meaning":"Active power injection at the substation bus 0, period t; the source notes P^S_{0,t} can be negative, representing back-feeding.","kind":"operational","domain":"Real-valued substation injection; the source assumes P^S_{i,t}=0 for all buses but the substation, while P^S_{0,t} may be less than 0.","unit":"kW","indices":["t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g01-E33","v141-b-g01-E12"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::x::v141-c-R001-X007"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R001-X008","symbol":"Q^S_{0,t}","meaning":"Reactive power injection at the substation bus 0, period t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Substation reactive injection; the source assumes Q^S_{i,t}=0 for all buses but the substation.","unit":"kvar","indices":["t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g01-E33","v141-b-g01-E12"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::x::v141-c-R001-X008"},{"variable_id":"var_beta","symbol":"\\beta","meaning":"Penalization factor in the compensation-and-penalty payment rule, chosen by the DSO.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; DSO-chosen scalar penalization factor","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E16"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_beta"},{"variable_id":"var_gamma","symbol":"\\gamma","meaning":"Per-unit coefficient of the quadratic deviation penalty, adjustable by the DSO.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; DSO-chosen scalar satisfying the stated incentive and participation trade-off","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g01-E25","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_gamma"},{"variable_id":"var_R_i","symbol":"R_i","meaning":"Fixed payment to aggregator i in ECPM, intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; fixed payment amount for aggregator i","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_R_i"},{"variable_id":"var_tildeK_i","symbol":"\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i","meaning":"Actual final flexibility profile implemented by aggregator i and measured by the DSO, possibly differing from the instruction.","kind":"operational","domain":"profile over t in \\Omega_T of measured actual flexibility factors; source does not state explicit numerical bounds for the measured profile","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E16"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_tildeK_i"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R001-T001","symbol":"\\hat{\\lambda}_{i,t}","meaning":"Declared flexibility cost parameter at node i, period t, submitted by aggregator i as part of its bid; it may differ from the actual \\lambda_{i,t}.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"$/kWh^2","indices":["i\\in\\Omega_b","t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E19"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::theta::v141-c-R001-T001"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R001-T002","symbol":"\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t}","meaning":"Declared maximum flexibility factor at node i, period t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["i\\in\\Omega_b","t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E30"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::theta::v141-c-R001-T002"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R001-T003","symbol":"\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}","meaning":"Declared minimum flexibility factor at node i, period t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["i\\in\\Omega_b","t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E30"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::theta::v141-c-R001-T003"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R001-T004","symbol":"P^D_{i,t}","meaning":"Active power demand at node i, period t, from the aggregator's expected demand profile communicated to the DSO.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kW","indices":["i\\in\\Omega_b","t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E31","v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g01-E32"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::theta::v141-c-R001-T004"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R001-T005","symbol":"Q^D_{i,t}","meaning":"Reactive power demand at node i, period t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kvar","indices":["i\\in\\Omega_b","t\\in\\Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g01-E32"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::theta::v141-c-R001-T005"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R001-T006","symbol":"R_{ij}","meaning":"Resistance of line ij.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"m\\Omega","indices":["ij\\in\\Omega_l"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E32"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::theta::v141-c-R001-T006"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R001-T007","symbol":"X_{ij}","meaning":"Reactance of line ij.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"m\\Omega","indices":["ij\\in\\Omega_l"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E32"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::theta::v141-c-R001-T007"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R001-T008","symbol":"\\overline V","meaning":"Maximum voltage magnitude.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kV","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E32"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::theta::v141-c-R001-T008"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R001-T009","symbol":"\\underline V","meaning":"Minimum voltage magnitude.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kV","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E32"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::theta::v141-c-R001-T009"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R001-T010","symbol":"\\overline I_{ij}","meaning":"Maximum current magnitude for line ij.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"A","indices":["ij\\in\\Omega_l"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E32"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::theta::v141-c-R001-T010"},{"parameter_id":"par_K_i_star","symbol":"\\mathbf K_i^*","meaning":"Optimal flexibility instruction communicated to aggregator i from the clearing problem using the declared tuples.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"clearing instruction"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E06"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_K_i_star"},{"parameter_id":"par_tilde_c_i","symbol":"\\tilde c_i","meaning":"Observed actual flexibility cost of aggregator i after the horizon, used as the actual-cost reimbursement term and in the maximum-cost penalty.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"observed actual cost"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g03-E15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_tilde_c_i"},{"parameter_id":"par_hatL_j","symbol":"\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j","meaning":"Declared parameter tuple of another aggregator j, used in the maximum-cost penalty term.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"declared parameter tuple of aggregator j"},"unit":null,"indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E06"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_hatL_j"},{"parameter_id":"par_c_j_eval","symbol":"c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)","meaning":"Declared flexibility cost of another aggregator j evaluated at its instruction and declared tuple in the maximum-cost penalty.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"declared cost evaluated at instruction"},"unit":null,"indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E11"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_c_j_eval"},{"parameter_id":"par_Omega_b","symbol":"\\Omega_b","meaning":"Set of nodes/aggregators over which the maximum-cost penalty and sums are indexed.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of nodes/aggregators"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E31"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_Omega_b"},{"parameter_id":"par_Omega_T","symbol":"\\Omega_T","meaning":"Set of time periods over which the quadratic deviation penalty is summed.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of time periods"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E31"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_Omega_T"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","stage":"L5","subtype":"network_constrained_local_clearing","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Clearing the low-voltage local market with the corresponding transmission-node TLMP given; the Caramanis scheme clears active power, reactive power, and reserve, and uses line and voltage limits to ensure distributed reserve deliverability.","form_kind":"procedural","identity_fields":["Object: local market resource allocation","Core operation: clear resources under feasible distribution-network conditions","Output: a local allocation satisfying the applicable network and delivery conditions"],"variation_fields":["Clearing entity: DSO","Market scope: low-voltage distribution network","External condition: the corresponding transmission-node TLMP is given","Product scope of the Caramanis scheme: active power, reactive power, and reserve","Feasibility conditions of the Caramanis scheme: line limits, voltage limits, and distributed reserve deliverability"],"constraint_refs":["18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002","18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001"],"paper_id":"18-dlmp_analysis","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.local","template_code":"JOC"},{"unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","stage":"L5","subtype":"minimax_cost_flexibility_allocation","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Jointly determine the flexibility allocation based on aggregator declarations, minimize the maximum declared cost, and output the instruction \\mathbf K_i^*. The implementation problem (11) minimizes z subject to z\\ge c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i) for all i, as well as constraints (1)–(3), (5)–(8), and (10), where the flexibility bounds use declared values.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: aggregator cross-period flexibility allocation","Core operation: minimize the maximum of the aggregators' declared costs within the common feasible region","Output: optimal flexibility instructions for each aggregator"],"variation_fields":["The organizer is the DSO; it is initiated when the network check fails and aggregator declarations have been obtained","The cost function is c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)=\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\hat\\lambda_{i,t}[P^D_{i,t}(1-K_{i,t})]^2","The declaration bounds are \\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\le K_{i,t}\\le\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t}","The original model (9) uses constraints (1)–(8); the implementation problem (11) introduces z and replaces (4) with the second-order cone inequality (10), retaining their respective feasible regions","Network conditions include power balance, node voltage bounds, and line current bounds"],"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C003","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C004","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C005","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C006","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001"],"paper_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.local","template_code":"JOC"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"18-dlmp_analysis::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","source_definition":"Clearing the low-voltage local market with the corresponding transmission-node TLMP given; the Caramanis scheme clears active power, reactive power, and reserve, and uses line and voltage limits to ensure distributed reserve deliverability.","retained_variants":["In the general narrative, the corresponding transmission-node TLMP is a given input, and the scope is the low-voltage local market.","Active power, reactive power, reserve, and distributed reserve deliverability are explicitly limited to the Caramanis scheme and are not generalized to all cited literature.","The nodal active-power DLMP is an accompanying price output; on that basis, price formation cannot be incorporated into the shared L5 operation.","No complete objective function or network equations are given, and this is not filled in as the minimax model of 40."],"constraint_refs":["18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002","18-dlmp_analysis::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001"]},{"unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","source_definition":"Jointly determine the flexibility allocation based on aggregator declarations, minimize the maximum declared cost, and output the instruction K_i^*. The implementation problem (11) minimizes z subject to z≥c_i(K_i,Ĺ_i) for all i, as well as constraints (1)–(3), (5)–(8), and (10), where the flexibility bounds use declared values.","retained_variants":["It is initiated when the network check fails and aggregator bids have been obtained; the object is demand-response flexibility and is not expanded to an arbitrary energy buying or selling role.","Retain min max_i c_i and the implementation problem min z, z≥c_i; the declared cost is Σ_t λ̂_{i,t}[P^D_{i,t}(1−K_{i,t})]^2.","Retain the declared lower and upper bounds K̂_lower≤K≤K̂_upper, the cross-period instructions, and the distinction between declared parameters and true parameters.","The original problem (9) uses constraints (1)–(8); the implementation problem (11) replaces equality (4) with cone inequality (10), and their feasible regions are not equivalent.","P, Q, V, and I are network configuration variables; this is not grounds to determine that this market trades reactive power or reserve products.","K_i^* is the clearing instruction, and the actual implemented quantity may differ from it; the CPM/ECPM formulas reference this output to calculate compensation and do not define a shared allocation operation."],"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C003","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C004","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C005","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C006","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008"]}],"source_parent_definition":{"definition":"The DSO directly incorporates distribution line and node voltage limits into local market resource allocation, and forms a clearing result that satisfies the corresponding model's network conditions based on the market inputs specified by the source.","object":"Local market resource allocation within the distribution network; the product and demand-response objects retain source-specific limitations.","inputs":"Line and voltage operating limits and the corresponding market inputs; 18 retains the given TLMP, and 40 retains demand forecasts, declared costs, and flexibility bounds.","operation":"In the allocation solution, line and voltage feasibility conditions are imposed simultaneously, and the source's product delivery requirements and economic criteria are enforced.","outputs":"Local resource allocation under the model's network conditions; 40 specifically outputs aggregator flexibility instructions.","boundary":"Limited to L5/JOC network-constrained clearing; excludes configurations with only internal energy conservation. The reserve deliverability requirement of 18 and the minimization of the maximum declared cost of 40 are retained separately; price inputs are not unified, and SOCP relaxation exactness or actual fulfillment is not guaranteed."},"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/50"});
